// PA-v0.21-012 (#1288): Bitfield read/write helpers (phase 1).
//
// Trait BitfieldOps declares two primitive shift-and-mask accessors that
// paper over the manual `word & ((1 << width) - 1) << start` juggling
// downstream code has been open-coding in every `bytes.pdx` consumer that
// touches an ACPI SDT header field, a NVMe CAP register, a MADT flags
// word, or a PCI capability slice.
//
// Phase 1 scope: two runtime-parametric helpers over a u64 word. The
// paideia-os R18-R28 build-out established empirically that shift-and-
// mask decode is proven-cheap when the ambient bitfield is under 64 bits
// (every "paper tiger" verdict at NVMe CAP, xHCI HCSPARAMS, VT-d ECAP,
// MADT flags leaned on this recipe); this trait cements the recipe as
// a first-class stdlib primitive so a downstream reviewer sees intent
// (`get_bits(cap, 0, 16)`) rather than an arithmetic incantation.
//
// Phase 2 (v0.22) will add `packed struct { field: u3, next: u5 }`
// syntax on top of these helpers; the accessors remain the underlying
// substrate.
//
// Semantics:
//   get_bits(word, start, width) -> u64
//       Returns (word >> start) & ((1 << width) - 1).
//       Zero-based bit indexing (bit 0 = LSB).
//       Precondition: start + width <= 64. width <= 63 (width == 64 is
//       caller-handled — pass width=63 and shift again, or read the word
//       directly). No runtime bounds check — costs a branch on every
//       accessor call, and the natural downstream sites are compile-time
//       known.
//
//   set_bits(word, start, width, val) -> u64
//       Returns word with bits [start, start+width) replaced by the low
//       `width` bits of val. Formally:
//         mask    = ((1 << width) - 1) << start
//         payload = (val << start) & mask
//         return  = (word & ~mask) | payload
//       The val-side mask is required so callers can pass any u64 as
//       val without concern for overflow into neighboring bits.
//       Same precondition on start + width <= 64 and width <= 63.
//
// Effect: !{} — pure arithmetic, no memory / MSR / port touch.
// Capability: none. Usable from any context, no `unsafe { }` required
// (the value-in-register lowering has no privileged encoding).

trait BitfieldOps {
  fn get_bits(word: u64, start: u32, width: u32) -> u64;
  fn set_bits(word: u64, start: u32, width: u32, val: u64) -> u64;
}
